      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Daptomycin, a large cyclic lipopeptide antibiotic, reached only about 5% of serum concentration in CSF in a rabbit meningitis model, supporting poor CNS penetration for large peptide antibiotics.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "Epimedin B, a large glycosylated natural product, was the least concentrated in rat brain and hardly passed the BBB, consistent with poor CNS access for large glycosylated molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is vancomycin, a large, highly polar glycopeptide antibiotic. The closest analogue oritavancin (Morgan similarity 0.85) shows only 1–5% CSF penetration in meningitis models, and related large peptide/glycopeptide antibiotics (dalbavancin, daptomycin) also show negligible CNS exposure. The few positive BBB results come from small flavonoid glycosides with very low similarity and are not transferable. Therefore, meaningful CNS access after systemic administration is unlikely.",
